WEBVTT

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:05.620
Hi, I'm Cao Jingyuan,
and this is Money in China,

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.980
my passion project for HY2206.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.690
This website is for students
who frequently use cash

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.720
or electronic payments but are
new to Chinese currency history.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.490
It connects historical objects
with something familiar,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.460
that is how we pay for things.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.390
The museum takes you through eight
periods, from early shells and different

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:35.380
coin shapes to imperial coins, paper
money and mobile payments in China today.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.920
My main message is that monetary
changes were shaped by practical needs,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.940
political decisions and trust.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.930
New forms could exist alongside older ones

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:50.580
because different transactions
called for different ways of paying.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:54.080
There are three ways
to explore the website.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.640
Let's begin with the 3D Museum.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.720
You can click the doorway, choose
a gallery from the Museum guide,

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.880
or start at the beginning here.

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.800
For example, I'm entering
Qin and Han Gallery.

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.140
You can click an object to face it,

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.640
then select Explore to open
its photograph and notes.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.720
This coin's square opening and inscription
gave us details to investigate.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:37.220
Click outside the panel, close it
and use the gallery controls to continue.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.860
If you want a shorter route,
you can use flashcards.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:50.680
Each card begins
with an object and a question.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:56.800
For example, this Eastern Zhou card
asks why money came in so many shapes.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.140
Turning it over reveals a short answer.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.470
Under Try it, you can zoom in and drag
these photographs of spade money

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.000
and knife money to examine their details.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:14.370
For more details, choose Read the history.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:17.690
This view brings together
the longer explanation,

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:21.050
historical text and photographs.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.870
You can jump to a period
using the timeline and

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:30.120
open More context
for additional discussion.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.960
The numbered references lead
to sources behind the claims.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:36.950
This makes it possible to follow

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:42.060
an explanation beyond the short object
notes and check the evidence yourself.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:47.740
The final section connects
the history with WeChat Pay

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.030
and Alipay in contemporary China.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:55.660
A quick scan can make paying feel
effortless, yet a payment still depends

00:02:55.690 --> 00:03:01.010
on available funds, someone accepting it
and services completing the transaction.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:06.640
Looking at older forms of money
helps us notice this arrangement

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:12.570
and understand why convenience depends
on more than what we see on the screen.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.090
You can also visit
My Journey and Reflections

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.940
to read about how the project
developed and what I learned.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.890
Start with whichever object
or period interests you

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.300
and choose the route that suits you.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.960
I hope you enjoy exploring the website.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:34.400
Thank you.
